                  "type": "concept",
                  "title": "AHIMA: The Association Behind Your Profession",
                  "content": "**AHIMA (American Health Information Management Association)** is the premier professional organization for Health Information Management. Understanding its history gives you context for everything you will study.\n\n**Founded in 1928 as ARLNA** (Association of Record Librarians of North America), the organization was created by the American College of Surgeons to standardize clinical records in hospitals. The original mission: ensure medical records were complete, accurate, and accessible for patient care.\n\n**Key milestones:**\n• 1928: ARLNA founded, focused on medical record standardization\n• 1938: Renamed to AMRL (American Medical Record Librarians)\n• 1970: Renamed to AMRA (American Medical Record Association)\n• 1991: Renamed to **AHIMA** — reflecting the shift from paper records to health information management\n\n**AHIMA's role today:**\n• Sets the **professional practice standards** for HIM\n• Develops and administers the **RHIT and RHIA certification exams**\n• Accredits HIM educational programs through **CAHIIM**\n• Publishes the *Journal of AHIMA* and the *Body of Knowledge (BoK)*\n• Advocates for HIM professionals in federal policy and regulation\n• Maintains the **HIIM domain framework** that defines professional competencies\n\nAHIMA has over **100,000 members** and is the single most important organization in your professional life. Your credential, your continuing education, and your professional identity all flow through AHIMA.",
